<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="500" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106073" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3.jpeg 1271w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3-300x179.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3-1024x611.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-3-768x458.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Back when I wrote eww, the Emacs web er wrangler?, I was futzing around a lot with basic input box functionality.  Like this:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="420" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106065" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1.jpeg 1369w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1-300x150.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1-1024x513.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-1-768x385.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And it basically kinda works, but it&#8217;s easy to break, even if you don&#8217;t really try.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="497" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106066" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2.jpeg 1362w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2-300x178.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2-1024x606.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-02-2-768x454.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>But that&#8217;s a bigger problem in <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot" data-cached-time="2024-07-02T14:14:42" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/ewpvJqI9m.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">user interfaces like this</a>, where you have all these boxes that can&#8217;t really grow in size, because that messes up the display.  But you have to be able to enter longer texts, so&#8230;</p>
<p><video controls loop muted><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Screencast-from-2024-07-02-14-02-18.webm" poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/posterVGSxfr001.png"  type="video/webm"></video></p>
<p>So I cheat, and move the second column down if the box gets too big.  Eh, whatchagonnado&#8230;</p>
<p>I briefly contemplated making a real &#8220;scrolling input box&#8221; &#8212; that is, if you type in too much, then the text at the left disappears from sight.  That&#8217;s easy enough to do, but then commands like `M-DEL&#8217; doesn&#8217;t work right, and you really want all those nice Emacs editing commands in input boxes.</p>
<p>Hm&#8230;  It occurs to me while typing this that perhaps this wouldn&#8217;t be impossible by judicious use of invisible text props &#8212; that is, if we&#8217;re typing too much, then make the left bits invisible so that it looks like it&#8217;s scrolling?  And then reveal it if moving the cursor to the left&#8230;  Urrr&#8230;</p>
<p>But!  I&#8217;ve fixed a whole bunch of other bugs in the input box things, and I&#8217;m thinking I should separate that out into a separate package, and then adapt eww to use that&#8230;  Possibly?</p>
<p>If it ever gets to a glitch-free state, that is.  It gets many things right now, but there&#8217;s a ton of corner cases.  It&#8217;s really fighting against the Emacs display/editing machinery &#8212; this is in a read-only buffer, but with inhibit-read-only text properties in the editable fields, and that&#8217;s not really&#8230;  er&#8230;  well thought through.  But I&#8217;ll try to keep poking at it and see what happens &#8212; I&#8217;ve already been through four different iterations of the algorithm (read the VC log and weep), and it&#8217;s just hard &#8212; Emacs kinda really rebels against altering the text too much from after-change-functions, so you can&#8217;t delete/reinsert the field, but have to pad things gingerly to avoid having point go awry&#8230;</p>
<p>I&#8217;m not sure this will ever actually &#8220;work&#8221;, as in &#8220;not have awful corner cases&#8221;.</p>
<p>Anyway.  I&#8217;ve implemented autocomplete in eplot for colours, faces, files and the like.</p>
<p>I think this interface for doing adjustments to the chart is getting to be kinda usable, actually.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08.jpeg" alt="" width="514" height="341" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106178" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08.jpeg 514w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-300x199.jpeg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 514px) 100vw, 514px" /></a></p>
<p>And I&#8217;ve implemented smoothed curves.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-03.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-03.jpeg" alt="" width="515" height="344" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106087" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-03.jpeg 515w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-03-300x200.jpeg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 515px) 100vw, 515px" /></a></p>
<p>Kids, don&#8217;t try Bezier curves at home &#8212; you may end up with something like the above if you&#8217;re not careful.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-1.jpeg" alt="" width="516" height="339" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106179" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-1.jpeg 516w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-1-300x197.jpeg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 516px) 100vw, 516px" /></a></p>
<p>JUST SAY NO!</p>
<p>As usual when I&#8217;m implementing something, I&#8217;m starting to notice how others actually do that thing I&#8217;m doing (so that I can steal I mean get inspired).</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07.png" alt="" width="840" height="588" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106174" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07.png 1000w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-300x210.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-768x538.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Here&#8217;s a chart from LanguageLog&#8230;</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="573" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106180" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2.jpeg 1253w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2-300x205.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2-1024x700.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-2-768x525.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And here&#8217;s how `M-x eplot&#8217; looks in the CSV buffer.  (Yes, you can now plot directly from CSV buffers if you have the pcsv buffer installed.)</p>
<p>It&#8217;s kinda reasonable?  Is the line-and-then-marker thing LanguageLog uses sensible?  I think it looks a bit messy, but it does mark every &#8220;real&#8221; data point, which is nice&#8230;  Hm.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="414" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106175" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13.jpeg 2262w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13-300x148.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13-1024x505.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13-768x379.jpeg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13-1536x757.jpeg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-07-13-2048x1010.jpeg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Here&#8217;s the same data from PubMed itself.  Notably, they include origo.  Both of these pick nice Y scales, but I guess it&#8217;s a matter of taste whether to include origo.</p>
<p><a href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="2560" height="2560" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2.jpeg" class="wp-image-106127" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2.jpeg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-300x300.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-1024x1024.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-150x150.jpeg 150w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-768x768.jpeg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-1536x1536.jpeg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-06-2-2048x2048.jpeg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /></a></p>
<p>This jazz chart reminded me that it should be possible to include formatting of the X and Y labels, so I added that:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-3.jpeg" alt="" width="512" height="342" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-106181" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-3.jpeg 512w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-08-3-300x200.jpeg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 512px) 100vw, 512px" /></a></p>
<p>And here&#8217;s a thing that&#8217;s usual in finance:</p>
<p><a href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1.jpg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="2333" height="1203" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1.jpg" class="wp-image-106102" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1.jpg 2333w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1-300x155.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1-1024x528.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1-768x396.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1-1536x792.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/2024-07-05-1-2048x1056.jpg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2333px) 100vw, 2333px" /></a></p>
<p>Plotting two different things with two different Y axes.  I&#8217;ve never liked this style &#8212; the two things plotted here have different scales, and I just think it&#8217;s harder to make sense of.  I suspect these charts became popular when you have to print a lot of charts on paper and wanted to save space, but that&#8217;s no longer usually a big concern (unless you&#8217;re publishing in a scienteriffic journal).</p>
<p>Also woth nothing here is that they chose pleasing max nunmbers (3 and 9) by extending the Y axes, and then they also did the same negatively&#8230;  which looks nice and symmetric, but wastes a lot of resolution.  And also &#8212; they chose horrible numbers between zero and max &#8212; 1.80, 3.60&#8230;!?</p>
<p>Anyway, I think eplot is getting close to actually being useful, so perhaps I&#8217;ll contemplate doing a release sometime soonish.  But you can test it out via <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot" data-cached-time="2024-07-08T14:29:33" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/ewpGotyvC.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a>.  Please do open issues if there&#8217;s something wrong, or you have ideas, or interesting data sets that eplot gets wrong.</p>
